usage:
The servers in the environment (with or without deployments), as JSON (id, name, hostname, description, tags,
status (draft or confirmed), revision, environment_ids).

Example: {"environment_id": "<environment id>"}

The deployments on a server or of a service (give one), as JSON: service, server, environment, runtime, port,
version, status (draft or confirmed) and revision.

Example: {"server_id": "<server id>", "service_id": null}

The connections from or to a service's deployments or one deployment (give one), as JSON: from and to
deployment, external service or host, port, protocol, description, status (draft or confirmed) and revision.

Example: {"service_id": "<service id>", "deployment_id": null}

The documents linked to a server, service or environment of this run and to the resources that belong to it (an environment its services and servers, a server its services, a service its code projects and servers), as JSON: each with id, title, description_short, tags, published (false: a new document this run drafted, not published yet) and your_draft (this run already has a draft of it). The resource's inventory document comes first. Read one with get_document. Look here before new_document, so you update a document instead of adding a second one.

Field: resource: {"type": "server" | "service" | "environment", "id": "<id>"}, or with "name" instead of "id" (a resource of this run: the Resource line, get_available_resources).

Example: {"resource": {"type": "service", "name": "api"}}
